-- id: vm-shared-path
-  label: vmmount
-  statement: A VM mount source outside the shared tree is refused; one inside it is permitted
-  law: R19
-  planes:
-  - ci
-  run:
-  - bin/vm-shared-path
-  fixtures:
-    must_fail: $OUTSIDE
-    must_pass: $HOME
-  cases:
-  - expect: pass
-    env:
-      VM_SHARED_ROOTS: $HOME
-    args:
-    - $HOME
-  # The refused argument is spelled `$OUTSIDE`, not a literal absolute path.
-  #
-  # WHY (incident 2026-09-13, CI run 34796984403). This case named
-  # `/private/tmp` as its argument, and the `vmmount` row went red on the Linux
-  # runner with `cites 1 thing(s) that do not exist: /private/tmp (no such
-  # path)`. `/private/tmp` is real on macOS -- it is what `/tmp` resolves to --
-  # and it does not exist on the runner, so the fixture was a claim about the
-  # machine the row was written on rather than about the gate.
-  #
-  # That reading also hid the more interesting half. `bin/vm-shared-path` reads
-  # `real=$(cd "$(dirname "$p")" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)/$(basename "$p")`, so
-  # when the parent does not exist the `cd` is swallowed and the refusal that
-  # follows is the refusal of a path that does not exist -- true, but not the
-  # property this row claims to grade. The run would have passed while grading
-  # nothing, which is the shape LAW 2 forbids. So the row now proves the gate
-  # with a path that exists on EVERY platform and is genuinely outside the
-  # shared root:
-  #
-  #   $OUTSIDE expands to `/` -- exists on macOS and on Linux, is not under
-  #   `$HOME`, and is named `..` from the shared root. The gate must refuse it
-  #   for being outside the tree.
-  #
-  # `$OUTSIDE` is resolved by bin/idp-rules at grade time, next to the existing
-  # `$HOME` expansion, so the row never carries a machine's own path in its text.
-  - expect: refuse
-    env:
-      VM_SHARED_ROOTS: $HOME
-      OUTSIDE: /
-    args:
-    - $OUTSIDE
-  ok: vm-shared-path permits a path under the shared root and refuses one outside it
-  fail: bin/vm-shared-path selftest
